From f112485b91a2ed52e6067faf8cdc55f7c6cf635e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Smart-Space <3180471716@qq.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 13:54:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Implement manual anti-aliasing for the Windows GDI back end

GDI has no anti-aliasing of its own: every path is rasterized with hard,
aliased edges.  This patch implements anti-aliasing by hand, using nothing
but plain GDI:

  1. the shape is drawn with ordinary GDI calls into an off-screen DIB
     that has "scale" times the resolution of the device,
  2. that DIB is box-filtered down to device resolution, which yields the
     exact area that the shape covers in every device pixel,
  3. the area is used as an alpha value when the shape is composited onto
     the destination.

The new file win/tkWinAA.c holds the engine; win/tkWinDraw.c routes
XFillArc, XDrawArc, XFillPolygon and XDrawLines through it while a
canvas with the new "-antialias" option is being redrawn.  Dashed lines,
stippled fills and non-copy raster operations keep using the plain path,
because their patterns are not scaled along with the shape.  Text is left
alone: GDI already anti-aliases TrueType glyphs by itself.
---
